  "text": "Two small, dry‑farmed wheat regions in a mountainous part of a western state are examined. One area covers roughly twenty square miles, the other about ten. Together they support about five hundred residents, a much higher density than the surrounding sparsely populated grazing and unused lands. In the larger area homes are spread fairly evenly, while in the smaller valley they line up along a single main road, a pattern that mirrors the differing road networks shaped by the local terrain. The flatter district follows a grid of roads and fences that line up with surveyed land sections, whereas the narrow valley has one principal route with a few side tracks leading into the foothills, and most dwellings sit close to that road. The mapped mile shows mostly level fields bounded by low metal fences, interspersed with a higher than usual amount of idle, pasture, and oak‑covered ground. An old farmstead has been deserted, its parcels now merged with neighboring farms. The wheat produced here is of excellent quality and commands strong demand, thanks to a cool early‑spring growth period, a hot dry summer, and the absence of fog that elsewhere favors barley cultivation."
